## Context

Ticket 0026 was ruled a monster on 2026-09-02 and decomposed by ladder rung;
this is the fifth child, and 0026 is the tracker. The RSS gate stands over
constraint C3, the memory constraint, rather than over any requirement: R20 and R26 were retired from the sheet on 2026-08-31 on the
criterion that what verifies a promise is not itself a promise. It has no rung
of its own for the same reason, which is why it is the one child of this split
scoped by instrument rather than by goal.

It is filed with the `needs-human` label because it is gated on a ruling, and
the ruling it is gated on is not the one 0026's body names. That body flags two
readings of R20's letter as awaiting ratification — the slow-suite placement and
the synthetic surrogate. **Both were ratified on 2026-08-29** (`DECISIONS.md`):
the budget assertion stays in `check-slow` with a cheap fixture asserting the
mechanism in the fast tier, and the deterministic synthetic monster at the
measured size satisfies R20's intent, conditional on revalidation against X3a at
each release. The awaiting entry that carried them was deleted on 2026-08-31,
having been answered twice. So that half no longer gates anything, and the
tracker's flag is stale.

What does gate it is newer. SPEC.md §5.2.9 leaves **three ceilings needing a
re-pin** after the 2026-09-02 topology, and says so rather than inventing them:
the server ceiling, priced on a process that loaded the query model and no
longer does; the pipeline ceiling, priced on a worker that loaded the passage
model and no longer does; and the embedding service, which has no ceiling at
all, being a class C3 did not have. §5.2.8's own gate text now says the gate
asserts whatever §5.2.9 says for all four roles rather than carrying its own
copy. Finding F1's collision between the pipeline ceiling and the multilingual
candidates may dissolve rather than be ruled, since it came from the worker
holding the model — a re-check and not yet a claim (0026's log,
2026-09-02T05:08Z).

**The ruling that unblocks this ticket**: a ratified re-pin, in `DECISIONS.md`,
of the three ceilings §5.2.9 names, together with the ceiling for the embedding
service class. Until then the gate can be built against the roles and the
assertions written, but it cannot assert a number, and a gate asserting an
unratified number is worse than one that reports it cannot look.

**Subjects and numbers are not the same lack**, recorded on ticket 0026 on
2026-09-02 by ticket 0550's reconciliation of the conductor lane and inherited
here, because that paragraph's home moved into this ticket when the tracker was
rewritten. The service clauses now have a SUBJECT: ticket 0575 builds the
embedding service boundary, so the cold-service p95 arm and the `loading` /
`absent` / `failed` status states the soak asserts have something to assert
against. Two of them still lack a NUMBER rather than a subject — the service
arm's ceiling, which does not exist at all and is ticket 0577's probe to size,
and the assertion on the pipeline worker, which is that the worker is KILLED at
its bound rather than that its peak sat under one, the bound itself being ticket
0554's to make real. Neither lack is a reason to defer writing the assertion;
both are reasons it cannot go green yet, which is exactly what the honest-red
rule above is for. That is also why this ticket carries no `Blocked-by` on
either of them: most of the gate — the surrogate, the transport clause, the
placement, the worker role — is buildable today, and a `Blocked-by` would make
the whole ticket unschedulable to protect two assertions that the honest-red
rule already protects.

## Relevant files

- `SPEC.md` §5.2.9, "**RAM**" — the process classes, the derivation, and the
  paragraph naming the three ceilings that need a re-pin. This section owns
  every number this gate asserts.
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.8, "**The RSS gate**" — the gate's own specification: the
  synthetic surrogate, the transport clause across both processes of the fetch
  path, and the rule that for the worker the gate asserts the kill and not the
  peak.
- `DECISIONS.md` — where the re-pin lands, and where the 2026-08-29 rulings on
  cadence and surrogate already are. Read, never edited by this ticket's work.
- `verification/SOLE-WRITER-0507.md` — finding F5, the no-materialize clause the
  transport clause instruments.
- `tickets/0025-experiments-x1-x7-each-before-its-depend.erg` — X3a, the
  real-document run on the author's machine that revalidates the surrogate at
  each release.

## Actions

1. Build the deterministic synthetic document to §5.2.8's specification —
   entry-structured, at the measured size, so the segmenter and the band cap are
   both exercised. It is deterministic so the fixture is a generator in the repo
   and not a committed blob.
2. The gate in its pre-machinery form: server-only against stock upstream at the
   reviewed SHA, red-with-waiver, the worker assertion joining once the
   scoped-issue machinery exists (tickets 0033, 0034, 0035).
3. The four roles, not two: server, conductor, pipeline worker, embedding
   service. Write each assertion against the role and leave its ceiling
   unresolved until the re-pin, reporting "no ratified ceiling" rather than a
   default.
4. For the worker, assert the **kill**: a decode driven past the bound ends the
   worker, not the machine. A peak assertion there would be asserting the wrong
   event.
5. The transport clause: resident memory across a fetch of the library's largest
   attachment, measured on both processes of the fetch path, because the clause
   it instruments is that no process on the path ever holds the document whole.
6. Placement per the 2026-08-29 ruling: the budget assertion in `check-slow`,
   a cheap fixture asserting the mechanism in the fast tier.
7. The revalidation clause: the surrogate carries a fidelity claim and only the
   library level renews it. Wire X3a's release-time revalidation as a stated
   condition of the gate's own green, not as a habit.

## Test

Red first, and here the red arm is the whole point of the instrument: drive a
decode past the bound and require the worker to die. A gate that has only ever
seen a well-behaved document cannot distinguish a bounded pipeline from an
unbounded one that happened not to be pushed.

The second control is the surrogate's own fidelity: the synthetic document must
reproduce the memory behaviour of the document class whose uncapped build was
measured, and the way to show it does is to run the uncapped path against the
surrogate and see the same shape. A surrogate that stays cheap under the
uncapped path is not standing in for anything.

## Verification

- [ ] The worker's kill arm was observed, not argued.
- [ ] The surrogate reproduces the uncapped behaviour of the class it stands in
      for.
- [ ] All four process roles have an assertion; the ones without a ratified
      ceiling report that, and do not pass.
- [ ] The transport clause measures both processes of the fetch path.
- [ ] No ceiling figure is written outside SPEC.md §5.2.9.
- [ ] `make check` green; `./tickets/erg check` green.

## Invariants

- The unit is the MUST clause, not the requirement: one assertion per clause, so
  README.md's `delivered` column becomes a count rather than a reader's
  judgement. Inherited from the tracker, ticket 0026.
- Honest red and waiver states in the Makefile, never a green that means "could
  not look". Inherited from the tracker, ticket 0026. The pre-machinery form is
  red-with-waiver and must read as red.
- Budgets live once, in SPEC.md §5.2.9. This ticket asserts by reference and
  copies nothing; the re-pin is a ruling in `DECISIONS.md` and then a number
  there, never a number here.
- The surrogate's fidelity is renewed by the library level or it stops meaning
  anything. That is the pattern every surrogate in the gate estate follows, not
  a special case of this one.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] the re-pin ratified in `DECISIONS.md` for the three ceilings §5.2.9 names,
      plus the embedding service's — the ruling that unblocks the rest
- [ ] the deterministic synthetic monster generated to §5.2.8's specification
- [ ] the gate asserts §5.2.9's budgets for all four process roles, by reference
- [ ] the worker's kill asserted and observed, not its peak, against the bound
      ticket 0554 makes real
- [ ] the service arm's ceiling asserted once ticket 0577's probe has sized it,
      reporting "no ratified ceiling" until then rather than a default
- [ ] the transport clause measured on both processes of the fetch path
- [ ] budget assertion in `check-slow`, mechanism fixture in the fast tier
- [ ] X3a's release-time revalidation wired as a condition of the gate's green
